{
  "id": "gern",
  "name": "like, love, hate + -ing",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "subtitle": "What do you like doing? After like, love and hate, use the -ing form of the next verb: I like swimming. I don't like cooking.",
  "briefMarks": ["like","love","hate","-ing form"],
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "subject"
      },
      {
        "text": "like / love / hate",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "verb + -ing",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "rest"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">like</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">playing</span> football. · She <span class=\"vp-verb\">doesn't like</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">cooking</span>. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Do</span> you <span class=\"vp-verb\">like</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">dancing</span>?",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>love</strong> is stronger than <strong>like</strong>. <strong>hate</strong> is the opposite of love. <em>I like swimming. We love dancing. He hates getting up early.</em>",
        "tag": "How much?"
      },
      {
        "html": "After <em>like, love, hate</em> the next verb ends in <strong>-ing</strong>: <em>I like reading books.</em> Not <em>I like read</em>. With a noun you do not need -ing: <em>I like pizza.</em>",
        "tag": "Verb + -ing"
      },
      {
        "html": "For he, she, it add <strong>-s</strong> to <em>like</em>: <em>She likes dancing.</em> For negatives, use <strong>don't / doesn't</strong>: <em>I don't like cooking. She doesn't like cooking.</em> Questions: <em>Do you like cooking? – Yes, I do.</em>",
        "tag": "Negatives and questions"
      },
      {
        "html": "How to make the <strong>-ing</strong> form: <em>play → playing</em>. Drop the -e: <em>make → making, dance → dancing</em>. Double the last letter after a short vowel: <em>swim → swimming, run → running</em>.",
        "tag": "Spelling"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>like</strong> says what you enjoy in general: <em>I like coffee.</em> <strong>would like</strong> says what you want now, politely: <em>I would like a coffee, please.</em> They are not the same.",
        "tag": "like ≠ would like"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Two common mistakes: ✗ <em>I like swim</em> → ✓ <em>I like swimming</em>. ✗ <em>I am like swimming</em> → ✓ <em>I like swimming</em>. <em>Yes, I do</em> is also a good answer to <em>Do you like...?</em> More about short answers at <a href=\"/yes-no-questions\">Yes/no questions</a>. Revise <a href=\"/present-simple\">Present simple</a> for <em>he likes</em> and <em>she doesn't like</em>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
